cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "skymatch: lost-in-space star identification with both error rates measured"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-02"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/OmAcharya-avtr/skymatch"
abstract: >-
  Lost-in-space star identification on a synthetic sky: power-law catalogue generation with
  close-pair preparation, a three-index pair table verified exactly against brute force,
  triangle matching and Pyramid fourth-star confirmation as vectorised joins, and attitude
  recovery by Davenport's q-method. Every operating point reports three exhaustive outcomes
  with Wilson intervals, so the false-identification rate is measured rather than assumed
  zero: the classical Pyramid rule made 0 false identifications in 660 trials (below 0.0058
  at 95 percent confidence) but 0.3833 with a 12-times-too-wide tolerance gate and four
  false detections. The dense-false-star failure regime is quantified to collapse, with the
  search ceiling reported alongside every rate. A learned candidate ranker is benchmarked
  against the classical rule on identical candidate lists, with its three no-gain regimes
  and its 28-times runtime penalty reported beside its wins. The catalogue is synthetic, so
  the results characterise the algorithms and do not predict on-sky performance.
  Research-grade; not flight-qualified. The learned model is not certified for operational
  flight use.
keywords:
  - star identification
  - star tracker
  - lost-in-space
  - pyramid algorithm
  - attitude determination
  - aerospace
